<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Qin</h1><p class="st-meta">384 CE – 417 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Qiang-ruled state of the Yao family at Chang'an, whose ruler Yao Xing brought Kumarajiva to translate the Buddhist scriptures into Chinese.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people">Qiang</a> general of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>, rebelled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> defeat in 383, captured and killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a> in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of Qin in 386, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a> defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a> remnants and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-qin">Western Qin</a>, made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang</a> submit and ruled much of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A devout Buddhist, he brought the Kuchean monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 401 and gave him a translation bureau with hundreds of assistants; their versions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> and other texts became standard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> was weakened by the rise of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helian-xia">Helian Xia</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and by succession disputes. In 417 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-hong">Yao Hong</a>, was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-qin.jpg" alt="Later Qin" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_404_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>384 CE – 417 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yao Chang, Yao Xing, Yao Hong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qin (Yao clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=400&amp;focus=later-qin" aria-label="Later Qin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Qin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L7855 1179M7769 1191L7713 1068M7769 1191L7696 1315"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7855 1179h0M7713 1068h0M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chang'an. <a href="/atlas?year=400&amp;focus=later-qin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
